course: dictionary
lesson: 6994
title: Parachutes and slow falls
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A parachute is a large piece of cloth fixed to a person or thing by strings. When it opens in the air, it makes them fall slowly and safely. «Blow a dandelion clock and each seed floats off on a tiny parachute.» Here the word is used for a seed, so a parachute does not have to be a person.
explain: As a verb, to parachute means to jump from a plane and come down with a parachute. «A falling object speeds up every second because gravity keeps pulling it; only air pushing back slows it, which is why a parachute falls gently and a stone does not.» The parachute lets the air push back hard enough to slow the fall.
explain: In a science lesson you can drop a paper parachute and time how long it takes to land. A bigger parachute falls more slowly. To explain why, say that air resistance, also called drag, pushes up on the cloth and slows the fall.
fact: A parachute opens in the air and makes things fall slowly and safely.
fact: Air resistance, also called drag, pushes up on a parachute and slows the fall.
fact: A bigger parachute falls more slowly than a smaller one.
fact: «Scientists found that a dandelion seed's parachute makes a little ring of swirling air above it that holds it up longer.»
fact: Parachute can be a noun (a parachute) or a verb (to parachute, parachuted, parachuting).
exercise: What does a parachute do when it opens in the air? | It makes things fall faster and faster | It stops the wind from blowing | It lights up the sky | *It makes things fall slowly and safely
exercise: «Blow a dandelion clock and each seed floats off on a tiny parachute.» Find and copy the word that means very small. | *tiny | blow | dandelion | floats
exercise: What pushes up on a parachute and slows the fall? | *Air resistance | A stone | Sunlight | Gravity
test: To parachute means what? | To drive a car very fast | To fold a sheet of paper | *To jump from a plane and come down with a parachute | To swim across a lake
test: Why does a parachute fall gently while a stone does not? | *Because air pushes back and slows the parachute | Because gravity stops pulling on the parachute | Because a stone is made of cloth | Because the parachute is heavier than the air
exam: Which sentence uses parachute as a verb? | A parachute is folded and packed away | Parachutes are sold in the shop | The parachute lay flat on the grass | *The team will parachute from the plane at noon
